WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:23.340
Croesus - All the Money in the World
Can money truly bring happiness?

00:00:23.340 --> 00:00:28.470
Well, if you ask King Croesus, then the answer
is a resounding “yes.”

00:00:28.470 --> 00:00:32.410
He ruled over the kingdom of Lydia at a time
when it was one of the wealthiest and most

00:00:32.410 --> 00:00:34.590
powerful kingdoms on the planet.

00:00:34.590 --> 00:00:39.079
He amassed untold riches the likes of which
the world had never seen.

00:00:39.079 --> 00:00:43.410
He spurred on the world economy by issuing
the first gold coins.

00:00:43.410 --> 00:00:46.950
And yet...it all came crashing down in the
end.

00:00:46.950 --> 00:00:51.920
Ever fickle, fortune abandoned Croesus and
he witnessed his once-mighty kingdom crumble

00:00:51.920 --> 00:00:57.140
to dust and become just a small part of an
even bigger, more powerful empire.

00:00:57.140 --> 00:01:02.359
And, if oracles are to be believed, this had
all been part of a prophecy over 100 years

00:01:02.359 --> 00:01:08.909
in the making as Croesus had been destined
to pay for the sins of his ancestors.

00:01:08.909 --> 00:01:15.520
The Kingdom of Lydia
The early years of Croesus will forever remain

00:01:15.520 --> 00:01:16.729
shrouded in mystery.

00:01:16.729 --> 00:01:22.030
As with many other ancient historical figures,
one of our main sources will, again, be Herodotus

00:01:22.030 --> 00:01:27.040
who actually focused the beginning of his
very first book in his Histories on Croesus,

00:01:27.040 --> 00:01:31.360
but even Herodotus did not have much to say
about the Lydian before he became king.

00:01:31.360 --> 00:01:35.850
And, as is always the case with these ancient
texts, there is going to be some legend mixed

00:01:35.850 --> 00:01:39.880
in with the truth, so have your pinch of salt
at the ready.

00:01:39.880 --> 00:01:46.070
Croesus was born circa 595 BC and would go
on to rule over Lydia, a kingdom located in

00:01:46.070 --> 00:01:49.650
Asia Minor, or Anatolia, as we know it today.

00:01:49.650 --> 00:01:55.680
The kingdom itself is an enigma, as no literature
or monuments with extensive inscriptions have

00:01:55.680 --> 00:01:56.680
survived.

00:01:56.680 --> 00:02:00.409
What we do know of the Lydians comes to us
second-hand from the Greeks.

00:02:00.409 --> 00:02:05.399
The name is derived from Lydus, an early king
of Lydia who ruled sometime during the 2nd

00:02:05.399 --> 00:02:06.649
millenium BC.

00:02:06.649 --> 00:02:11.390
He was part of the first of three dynasties
that ruled over the kingdom - the Maeoniae.

00:02:11.390 --> 00:02:15.500
How many there were, how long they ruled,
how they lost power - we have no idea, but

00:02:15.500 --> 00:02:20.140
at one point they were replaced by the Heraclid
Dynasty, so called because they claimed to

00:02:20.140 --> 00:02:22.160
be descendants of Hercules.

00:02:22.160 --> 00:02:26.470
About them we have a little more information
thanks again to good, ol’ reliable Herodotus.

00:02:26.470 --> 00:02:34.840
He said that the Heraclids ruled over Lydia
for 22 generations, or 505 years, with a son

00:02:34.840 --> 00:02:36.770
succeeding his father each time.

00:02:36.770 --> 00:02:42.860
We only know the names of three of them, but
relevant to us was only the last King Candaules,

00:02:42.860 --> 00:02:44.640
also known as Myrsilus to the Greeks.

00:02:44.640 --> 00:02:49.140
The story of how he lost power is a fascinating
one that merits repeating, even if it might

00:02:49.140 --> 00:02:51.260
be just a legend.

00:02:51.260 --> 00:02:55.800
After all, it is not every day that a powerful
dynasty is brought down by voyeurism.

00:02:55.800 --> 00:03:00.840
Candaules had a beautiful wife named Nyssia
and she wasn’t just beautiful, she was apparently

00:03:00.840 --> 00:03:03.920
one of the most stunning creatures to ever
walk the earth.

00:03:03.920 --> 00:03:05.700
At least, that was what Candaules thought.

00:03:05.700 --> 00:03:10.310
At first, you might think this was rather
sweet, a man so deeply enamored with his wife.

00:03:10.310 --> 00:03:15.280
But the king raved about Nyssia’s beauty
to anyone and everyone who would listen, particularly

00:03:15.280 --> 00:03:18.320
his bodyguard and most trusted servant, Gyges.

00:03:18.320 --> 00:03:22.770
One day, the king was going on and on about
his wife to Gyges and, looking at his bodyguard,

00:03:22.770 --> 00:03:24.590
he detected a whiff of skepticism.

00:03:24.590 --> 00:03:29.290
Because, as Candaules thought, a man trusts
his eyes more than his ears, the king decided

00:03:29.290 --> 00:03:34.150
that Gyges must see Nyssia naked so that he
may be convinced that she was, indeed, the

00:03:34.150 --> 00:03:35.930
most beautiful woman in the world.

00:03:35.930 --> 00:03:40.180
Gyges was shocked when he heard and pleaded
with the king but, at the end of the day,

00:03:40.180 --> 00:03:44.550
Candaules was his master so, if he wished
it, it had to be done.

00:03:44.550 --> 00:03:48.660
That night, the king took his bodyguard into
the royal chambers and had him hide behind

00:03:48.660 --> 00:03:49.660
a door.

00:03:49.660 --> 00:03:52.860
Nyssia came in, took off her clothes and got
in bed with her husband.

00:03:52.860 --> 00:03:57.240
Gyges witnessed the whole thing and then tried
to sneak out, but the queen saw him and instantly

00:03:57.240 --> 00:03:58.750
realized what had happened.

00:03:58.750 --> 00:04:02.580
Nyssia felt deeply ashamed and decided that
she needed vengeance.

00:04:02.580 --> 00:04:07.490
The next day, she called Gyges to her, and
he came voluntarily because he had not realized

00:04:07.490 --> 00:04:09.340
yet that he had been spotted the night before.

00:04:09.340 --> 00:04:15.030
Nyssia gave him a choice: either kill himself
or kill his master, become king and take her

00:04:15.030 --> 00:04:16.150
as his new wife.

00:04:16.150 --> 00:04:20.430
So his options were to die or to become rich
and powerful and marry one of the most beautiful

00:04:20.430 --> 00:04:21.430
women in the world.

00:04:21.430 --> 00:04:25.780
It was a hard decision but, in the end, Gyges
went with option B.

00:04:25.780 --> 00:04:30.250
The queen brought him into her sleeping chambers
just like the king did the night before.

00:04:30.250 --> 00:04:34.100
The bodyguard waited for his master to fall
asleep and stabbed him with a dagger.

00:04:34.100 --> 00:04:37.370
Gyges took over the throne and founded the
Mermnad Dynasty.

00:04:37.370 --> 00:04:42.020
It should be noted that this is just one of
multiple stories of how the Heraclids fell.

00:04:42.020 --> 00:04:47.010
In another version by Plato, Gyges was a shepherd
who took power with the help of a magic ring

00:04:47.010 --> 00:04:50.970
that made him invisible, but that one is a
little bit unlikelier.

00:04:50.970 --> 00:04:55.470
Some modern historians adopt a more practical
viewpoint and believe that Candaules was simply

00:04:55.470 --> 00:04:58.430
assassinated because he had become unpopular
with the people.

00:04:58.430 --> 00:05:03.120
Unsurprisingly, not everyone was happy with
the slaying of Candaules and his death prompted

00:05:03.120 --> 00:05:04.350
a minor civil war.

00:05:04.350 --> 00:05:08.430
However, Nyssia managed to quell the uprising
pretty fast by decreeing that they should

00:05:08.430 --> 00:05:10.940
all heed the decision of the oracle of Delphi.

00:05:10.940 --> 00:05:15.940
If the seer ordained Gyges to be the new king
of the Lydians, then he would assume power.

00:05:15.940 --> 00:05:18.430
If not, then it went back to the Heraclids.

00:05:18.430 --> 00:05:23.750
The oracle sided with Gyges, but divined that
the Heraclids would have their vengeance after

00:05:23.750 --> 00:05:25.500
five generations.

00:05:25.500 --> 00:05:29.630
The Mermnadae ruled Lydia with great success
for 140 years.

00:05:29.630 --> 00:05:34.770
During the reign of the fourth king, Alyattes,
the kingdom covered most of western Asia Minor.

00:05:34.770 --> 00:05:42.060
But in 560 BC Alyattes died and he was succeeded
by his son, the fifth generation Mermnad,

00:05:42.060 --> 00:05:43.910
Croesus.

00:05:43.910 --> 00:05:50.500
King Croesus
Alyattes was a very powerful and successful

00:05:50.500 --> 00:05:53.500
king who ruled for almost 60 years.

00:05:53.500 --> 00:05:57.970
During that time, he waged many wars to increase
the reach of his kingdom, particularly against

00:05:57.970 --> 00:06:00.030
the Ionian and Aeolian Greek city-states in
Asia Minor.

00:06:00.030 --> 00:06:03.810
To the east of the kingdom was the Median
Empire who also fought against the Lydians.

00:06:03.810 --> 00:06:08.780
However, in 585 BC, while the two kingdoms
were warring, there was a solar eclipse today

00:06:08.780 --> 00:06:11.020
known as the Eclipse of Thales.

00:06:11.020 --> 00:06:14.810
Both sides took it as an omen and declared
a truce soon after.

00:06:14.810 --> 00:06:18.560
After a treaty was signed, the Halys River
became the official border between the two

00:06:18.560 --> 00:06:23.000
empires and the two kingdoms even arranged
a royal marriage to help solidify this new

00:06:23.000 --> 00:06:24.000
peace.

00:06:24.000 --> 00:06:27.950
The Lydian princess offered for marriage was
Croesus’s older sister, Aryenis, who married

00:06:27.950 --> 00:06:30.270
the King of Media, Astyages.

00:06:30.270 --> 00:06:34.960
Croesus did not know it yet, but this new
relation would later force him into a war

00:06:34.960 --> 00:06:36.960
that would bring on his doom.

00:06:36.960 --> 00:06:39.660
Croesus’s rise to the throne was not without
incident.

00:06:39.660 --> 00:06:43.490
He had clearly been positioned as his father’s
heir when he was given the governorship of

00:06:43.490 --> 00:06:45.920
an important city called Adramyttium.

00:06:45.920 --> 00:06:50.650
This post was traditionally assigned to the
successor so that he may receive some administrative

00:06:50.650 --> 00:06:53.330
experience before assuming the mantle of king.

00:06:53.330 --> 00:06:58.000
However, when Alyattes died and Lydia was
getting ready to accept Croesus as its new

00:06:58.000 --> 00:07:02.790
king, his authority was challenged by a half-brother
named Pantaleon.

00:07:02.790 --> 00:07:08.419
At one point, Croesus was even the target
of a conspiracy and barely escaped being poisoned

00:07:08.419 --> 00:07:09.870
by his stepmother.

00:07:09.870 --> 00:07:14.430
We do not know the details of this sibling
rivalry but, obviously, in the end, Croesus

00:07:14.430 --> 00:07:18.669
prevailed and assumed kingship of Lydia when
he was 35 years old.

00:07:18.669 --> 00:07:22.690
As soon as he was certain in his role, King
Croesus resumed the favorite habit of his

00:07:22.690 --> 00:07:25.070
father, which was to fight his neighbors.

00:07:25.070 --> 00:07:30.169
His first target was Ephesus, a Greek city
on the Ionian coast, located a couple of miles

00:07:30.169 --> 00:07:33.050
from where the modern Turkish city of Selçuk
is today.

00:07:33.050 --> 00:07:37.730
The city’s main claim to fame was its temple
dedicated to the goddess Artemis which had

00:07:37.730 --> 00:07:41.120
been destroyed by a flood sometime during
the 7th century BC.

00:07:41.120 --> 00:07:47.520
Croesus conquered Ephesus around 560 BC and
he sponsored, at least partially, the rebuilding

00:07:47.520 --> 00:07:51.370
of the Temple of Artemis, making it bigger
and more opulent than before.

00:07:51.370 --> 00:07:56.060
It was described as being over 330 feet long,
having 40-ft tall columns, and being made

00:07:56.060 --> 00:07:57.160
mostly of marble.

00:07:57.160 --> 00:08:01.450
This, of course, would go on to be known as
one of the Seven Wonders of the World but,

00:08:01.450 --> 00:08:05.900
by the time such lists started making the
rounds in the Greek world, the temple had

00:08:05.900 --> 00:08:08.930
actually been destroyed and rebuilt once more.

00:08:08.930 --> 00:08:12.970
Around the mid 4th century BC, a man named
Herostratus set it on fire.

00:08:12.970 --> 00:08:17.900
The arsonist was obsessed with fame and wanted
people to know his name at all costs.

00:08:17.900 --> 00:08:21.780
What better way than to destroy one of the
most beautiful things in the world?

00:08:21.780 --> 00:08:27.560
The Ephesians caught, tortured and executed
Herostratus, but also sentenced him to damnatio

00:08:27.560 --> 00:08:31.990
memoriae, the “condemnation of memory”
where he was stricken from every official

00:08:31.990 --> 00:08:35.040
record so that his name may be forgotten by
history.

00:08:35.040 --> 00:08:38.650
After all, what better punishment could there
be for someone who wanted to be remembered

00:08:38.650 --> 00:08:40.150
at all costs?

00:08:40.150 --> 00:08:44.960
Unfortunately for the Ephesians, their plan
did not work as a Greek historian named Theopompus

00:08:44.960 --> 00:08:49.450
recorded the event and today we still remember
the name and actions of Herostratus.

00:08:49.450 --> 00:08:55.420
Back to Croesus, once Ephesus was conquered,
he simply moved on from one target to the

00:08:55.420 --> 00:08:56.420
next.

00:08:56.420 --> 00:09:00.110
He didn’t rest until all the Greek cities
on mainland Asia Minor were either part of

00:09:00.110 --> 00:09:02.460
his kingdom or paid him tribute.

00:09:02.460 --> 00:09:06.940
So it happened that Croesus subjected all
the people west of the Halys River except

00:09:06.940 --> 00:09:09.250
for the Cilicians and Lycians.

00:09:09.250 --> 00:09:13.570
Afterwards, he shifted his focus to the nearby
islands in the Aegean Sea and intended to

00:09:13.570 --> 00:09:14.800
conquer them, as well.

00:09:14.800 --> 00:09:16.950
In the end, though, he decided against it.

00:09:16.950 --> 00:09:21.190
The Lydian army was famed for its horseback
soldiers who, of course, would be completely

00:09:21.190 --> 00:09:23.010
useless in naval warfare.

00:09:23.010 --> 00:09:30.520
Instead, Croesus made friends with the Greek
island nations.

00:09:30.520 --> 00:10:36.190
Toss a Coin to Your King
Unsurprisingly, all

00:10:36.190 --> 00:11:09.930
his

00:11:09.930 --> 00:11:12.140
conquests made Croesus a very rich man.

00:11:12.140 --> 00:11:15.120
In fact, he was probably among the richest
men in history.

00:11:15.120 --> 00:11:19.030
Besides the tributes he received from the
Greek cities, the king’s vast wealth also

00:11:19.030 --> 00:11:23.750
came from abundant mineral deposits located
throughout Lydia, particularly in Sardis,

00:11:23.750 --> 00:11:24.870
the kingdom’s capital.

00:11:24.870 --> 00:11:29.930
The expression “as rich as Croesus” is
still in use today, thousands of years after

00:11:29.930 --> 00:11:30.930
he died.

00:11:30.930 --> 00:11:35.200
His massive wealth aside, Croesus had another
claim to fame which ensured his role in the

00:11:35.200 --> 00:11:38.070
history of economics - the introduction of
coins.

00:11:38.070 --> 00:11:41.820
The Lydian king is credited with issuing the
first ever gold and silver coins.

00:11:41.820 --> 00:11:46.880
These had a standardized purity and a fixed
exchange rate between various denominations

00:11:46.880 --> 00:11:49.529
and between the gold and silver variants.

00:11:49.529 --> 00:11:54.370
These coins were called kroiseioi stateres
in ancient documents, although today we simply

00:11:54.370 --> 00:11:56.460
refer to them as Croeseids.

00:11:56.460 --> 00:11:58.430
They depicted a lion and a bull.

00:11:58.430 --> 00:12:03.709
The use of coins, period, is generally ascribed
to Lydia, although about a hundred years or

00:12:03.709 --> 00:12:05.900
so before the introduction of Croeseids.

00:12:05.900 --> 00:12:10.680
This claim is a bit more controversial, though,
as some historical texts say that other places

00:12:10.680 --> 00:12:13.330
were first, although still in the same region.

00:12:13.330 --> 00:12:18.470
Some ancient writers claimed the city of Argos
used coins first, or the Greek island of Aegina

00:12:18.470 --> 00:12:21.720
which had the silver stater depicting a turtle.

00:12:21.720 --> 00:12:26.520
If the Lydians were not first, they were definitely
among the earliest adopters.

00:12:26.520 --> 00:12:31.390
Their original coins, just like the Greek
staters, were made out of electrum - an alloy

00:12:31.390 --> 00:12:33.520
of gold and silver that occurs naturally.

00:12:33.520 --> 00:12:38.450
These coins were definitely in use during
the reign of Alyattes, but they were not practical

00:12:38.450 --> 00:12:40.870
enough for day-to-day spending.

00:12:40.870 --> 00:12:44.140
Even the smallest denominations were still
too valuable to be used to buy something trivial

00:12:44.140 --> 00:12:46.660
like a loaf of bread, for example.

00:12:46.660 --> 00:12:49.020
This changed during Croesus’s time.

00:12:49.020 --> 00:12:53.260
According to Herodotus, again, he was not
only the first to introduce gold and silver

00:12:53.260 --> 00:12:56.550
coins, but also the first to use them to sell
goods by retail.

00:12:56.550 --> 00:13:01.480
While the original Lydian coins weighed above
10 grams, Croesus issued a wide range of small

00:13:01.480 --> 00:13:05.280
denominations, going all the way to 1/48th
of a gram.

00:13:05.280 --> 00:13:08.750
This made it possible for Lydians to use coins
in their daily shopping trips.

00:13:08.750 --> 00:13:12.700
And in case you were wondering how the Lydians
determined the purity of a coin, they used

00:13:12.700 --> 00:13:14.420
a tool called a touchstone.

00:13:14.420 --> 00:13:19.260
It was simply a piece of dark stone made out
of slate, or fieldstone, or other rocks with

00:13:19.260 --> 00:13:22.540
a finely grained surface on which soft metals
left a trace.

00:13:22.540 --> 00:13:26.640
They took a coin with an unknown gold purity
and rubbed it against the touchstone so it

00:13:26.640 --> 00:13:27.640
left a streak.

00:13:27.640 --> 00:13:31.750
They then compared the streak to others made
with coins of known purity and simply looked

00:13:31.750 --> 00:13:32.930
for a color match.

00:13:32.930 --> 00:13:37.560
The fact that Croesus was a conquering warlord
only served to expedite the spread of coins.

00:13:37.560 --> 00:13:40.820
They were used in all the Greek cities he
subjected.

00:13:40.820 --> 00:13:44.580
From the Greeks, it spread to the Romans who
spread it to the whole of Europe.

00:13:44.580 --> 00:13:48.280
When Lydia was conquered by the Persians,
it spread to Mesopotamia.

00:13:48.280 --> 00:13:52.970
When the Persians were conquered by Alexander
the Great, he spread it even further and so

00:13:52.970 --> 00:13:57.750
it went until coin usage became a standard
across the globe.

00:13:57.750 --> 00:14:05.240
A Visitor from Greece
Croesus seemed to live by the creed of “if

00:14:05.240 --> 00:14:07.580
you got it, then flaunt it.”

00:14:07.580 --> 00:14:11.519
After all, what was the point of being the
richest man in the world if he couldn’t

00:14:11.519 --> 00:14:13.130
show off his vast wealth?

00:14:13.130 --> 00:14:17.650
He received many esteemed guests, particularly
from the Greek world (that is, the part of

00:14:17.650 --> 00:14:19.550
the Greek world that he hadn’t conquered).

00:14:19.550 --> 00:14:24.670
Aesop, the fabled fable writer was among his
visitors, but the most notable guest to Lydia

00:14:24.670 --> 00:14:29.460
was Solon, the Athenian statesman who reformed
the city’s laws and set the foundations

00:14:29.460 --> 00:14:31.110
for Athenian democracy.

00:14:31.110 --> 00:14:35.570
After he finished his administrative duties,
Solon left Athens and went on a 10-year trip

00:14:35.570 --> 00:14:36.570
to see the world.

00:14:36.570 --> 00:14:40.530
Towards the end, he arrived in Sardis where
he was graciously greeted by King Croesus

00:14:40.530 --> 00:14:42.450
who rolled out the welcome wagon.

00:14:42.450 --> 00:14:46.600
Solon was given a tour of the palace where
he saw all the gold, the silver, the precious

00:14:46.600 --> 00:14:49.740
stones, and other vast riches that Croesus
possessed.

00:14:49.740 --> 00:14:54.339
Afterwards, the confident king asked his wise
visitor whom he considered to be “blessed

00:14:54.339 --> 00:14:55.670
beyond all men.”

00:14:55.670 --> 00:14:59.050
The answer probably surprised and even annoyed
the Lydian king.

00:14:59.050 --> 00:15:03.839
Surely, he was expecting Solon to say that
“Croesus was blessed beyond all others”,

00:15:03.839 --> 00:15:04.920
but he didn’t.

00:15:04.920 --> 00:15:08.720
The philosopher concluded that a man named
Tellus of Athens had that honor.

00:15:08.720 --> 00:15:12.670
He reasoned that Tellus had a good family
full of noble sons and healthy grandchildren,

00:15:12.670 --> 00:15:17.709
and, in his old age, he enjoyed a glorious
death in battle, securing a victory for the

00:15:17.709 --> 00:15:20.440
Athenians against their neighbors at Eleusis.

00:15:20.440 --> 00:15:24.950
Okay, so Croesus wasn’t first, but surely
he would claim second place, right?

00:15:24.950 --> 00:15:28.720
Well, not according to Solon who believed
that runners-up were two brothers from Argos

00:15:28.720 --> 00:15:30.220
named Cleobis and Biton.

00:15:30.220 --> 00:15:34.450
These two had a mother named Cydippe who was
a priestess of Hera.

00:15:34.450 --> 00:15:38.800
One day, she needed to get into town for a
festival dedicated to the goddess, but could

00:15:38.800 --> 00:15:40.870
not find the oxen to pull her cart.

00:15:40.870 --> 00:15:45.350
Instead, her loyal sons yolked themselves
and pulled the cart for six miles into Argos.

00:15:45.350 --> 00:15:48.940
When they arrived in the city, the Argives
gave the brothers a hero’s welcome for their

00:15:48.940 --> 00:15:51.350
dedication to their mother and to Hera.

00:15:51.350 --> 00:15:55.250
Cleobis and Biton then made sacrifices to
the gods, ate plentifully and fell asleep

00:15:55.250 --> 00:15:56.339
in the temple.

00:15:56.339 --> 00:16:01.529
Cydippe prayed to Hera that her sons may receive
the greatest boon for the honor they showed

00:16:01.529 --> 00:16:03.130
and the goddess agreed.

00:16:03.130 --> 00:16:07.810
Cleobis and Biton died peacefully in their
sleep and were allowed to rest for eternity.

00:16:07.810 --> 00:16:12.320
In the end, Solon reasoned that he could not
count Croesus among the truly blessed until

00:16:12.320 --> 00:16:14.320
he knew that the king died well.

00:16:14.320 --> 00:16:18.740
Unsurprisingly, this angered Croesus who sent
Solon away without any lavish gifts, concluding

00:16:18.740 --> 00:16:21.810
that the old man was a fool for ignoring his
wealth.

00:16:21.810 --> 00:16:25.329
Basically, there were two lessons to take
from Solon’s stories.

00:16:25.329 --> 00:16:30.190
One - that money wasn’t everything; and
two - that fortune can be fickle and change

00:16:30.190 --> 00:16:31.190
suddenly.

00:16:31.190 --> 00:16:35.160
Croesus, of course, immediately dismissed
both lessons, but he would eventually learn

00:16:35.160 --> 00:16:36.660
them the hard way.

00:16:36.660 --> 00:16:41.950
Soon after Solon departed Sardis, Croesus
had a dream which showed that his son, Atys,

00:16:41.950 --> 00:16:44.090
would be killed by an iron spear.

00:16:44.090 --> 00:16:46.220
Atys was the heir to the Lydian Kingdom.

00:16:46.220 --> 00:16:51.130
Croesus had another son, but he was deaf and
dumb and Herodotus didn’t even bother naming

00:16:51.130 --> 00:16:52.130
him.

00:16:52.130 --> 00:16:56.440
Fearing this vision, the king forbade Atys
from leading any Lydian armies into battle.

00:16:56.440 --> 00:17:00.780
One day, he received a plea for help from
the province of Mysia which was being attacked

00:17:00.780 --> 00:17:04.410
by a giant boar that destroyed the fields
and killed the livestock.

00:17:04.410 --> 00:17:07.579
Croesus allowed Atys to lead an expedition
to slay the beast.

00:17:07.579 --> 00:17:11.039
After all, it was unlikely that the boar would
wield a spear.

00:17:11.039 --> 00:17:16.130
Part of that expedition was a man named Adrastus,
a former prince from Phrygia who had been

00:17:16.130 --> 00:17:20.319
banished by the king after he accidentally
spilled the blood of his brother.

00:17:20.319 --> 00:17:26.270
As Phrygia was subjected to Lydia, King Croesus
took Adrastus in and purified him of his blood

00:17:26.270 --> 00:17:27.270
crime.

00:17:27.270 --> 00:17:31.970
In exchange, he tasked Adrastus to act as
Atys’s bodyguard and make sure that no harm

00:17:31.970 --> 00:17:32.970
came to him.

00:17:32.970 --> 00:17:35.610
He agreed, but ended up doing the exact opposite.

00:17:35.610 --> 00:17:40.120
During the fight with the boar, Adrastus threw
a spear that missed the animal but hit and

00:17:40.120 --> 00:17:41.139
killed Atys.

00:17:41.139 --> 00:17:45.640
The king cried and mourned his son, lamenting
that his mighty kingdom no longer had an heir.

00:17:45.640 --> 00:17:50.139
However, soon enough, he would have bigger
problems to face as Cyrus the Great was on

00:17:50.139 --> 00:17:53.019
the warpath.

00:17:53.019 --> 00:17:59.159
Croesus Fights Cyrus
You might recall we said earlier that Lydia

00:17:59.159 --> 00:18:02.730
had an alliance by marriage with the Median
Empire to the east.

00:18:02.730 --> 00:18:06.340
Well, in 550 BC, the Median Empire didn’t
exist anymore.

00:18:06.340 --> 00:18:11.029
It had been conquered by Cyrus the Great and
added to his Achaemenid Empire.

00:18:11.029 --> 00:18:15.259
Just like Croesus and many other warlords
before him, Cyrus had a lust for power and

00:18:15.259 --> 00:18:16.649
wanted to grow his empire.

00:18:16.649 --> 00:18:20.590
He subjugated all the lands in his path and
it was clear to everyone that he was on a

00:18:20.590 --> 00:18:25.600
collision course with the Lydian Kingdom as
one of the only other major powers in the

00:18:25.600 --> 00:18:26.600
region.

00:18:26.600 --> 00:18:30.110
Plus, as the Median queen was Croesus’s
sister, he could not leave this provocation

00:18:30.110 --> 00:18:31.110
unanswered.

00:18:31.110 --> 00:18:35.100
By the way, if you would like more detail
on the events, we already did a full video

00:18:35.100 --> 00:18:40.840
on Cyrus the Great so you can check the link
and learn all about the man who became King

00:18:40.840 --> 00:18:42.730
of the Four Corners of the World.

00:18:42.730 --> 00:18:47.840
Croesus didn’t want to act rash so, before
he took any course of action, he wanted to

00:18:47.840 --> 00:18:49.119
consult the oracles.

00:18:49.119 --> 00:18:52.389
However, the question was which oracle should
he trust?

00:18:52.389 --> 00:18:55.889
As a test, he sent agents to all the oracles
in the land.

00:18:55.889 --> 00:19:00.269
They all left the same day and were to inquire
what King Croesus would be doing 100 days

00:19:00.269 --> 00:19:01.269
later.

00:19:01.269 --> 00:19:04.899
Out of them all, only two gave answers that
satisfied the king - the oracle of Amphiaraus

00:19:04.899 --> 00:19:06.619
and the oracle of Delphi.

00:19:06.619 --> 00:19:11.450
The latter was the same oracle that ordained
Gyges to rule Lydia and also predicted the

00:19:11.450 --> 00:19:14.129
fall of his dynasty after five generations.

00:19:14.129 --> 00:19:18.070
We don’t know what the Amphiaraus oracle
said, but the Delphian seer who was called

00:19:18.070 --> 00:19:22.590
the Pythia correctly predicted that on the
hundredth day, King Croesus would eat tortoise

00:19:22.590 --> 00:19:25.529
and lamb meat boiled in a bronze cauldron.

00:19:25.529 --> 00:19:29.669
After this prediction came to pass, Croesus
made lavish offerings to the temple at Delphi.

00:19:29.669 --> 00:19:34.720
He wanted to know if he should attack Cyrus
and the Pythia gave him, perhaps, the most

00:19:34.720 --> 00:19:36.860
notorious divination in history.

00:19:36.860 --> 00:19:41.460
She predicted that, if Croesus attacked Cyrus,
he would destroy a great empire.

00:19:41.460 --> 00:19:46.600
Immediately, he assumed this could only mean
the Persian Empire so Lydia went to war.

00:19:46.600 --> 00:19:50.240
As Croesus would later discover, the oracle
was talking about his own.

00:19:50.240 --> 00:19:54.519
The prophetess also gave the king some practical
advice and told him to seek out the mightiest

00:19:54.519 --> 00:19:56.629
of Greeks and make them his friends.

00:19:56.629 --> 00:19:58.909
That would be Sparta.

00:19:58.909 --> 00:20:04.150
In the end, Lydia allied with the Spartans,
but also with the Egyptians and the Babylonians.

00:20:04.150 --> 00:20:07.649
How Croesus got each one to join his war against
Cyrus, we’re not sure.

00:20:07.649 --> 00:20:11.399
They were probably all paid off, but some
would have also been concerned with the danger

00:20:11.399 --> 00:20:14.950
posed by the Persian Empire growing uninhabited
in their vicinity.

00:20:14.950 --> 00:20:17.610
Ultimately, the allies made little difference
for Croesus.

00:20:17.610 --> 00:20:22.970
First, he and Cyrus fought to a stalemate
at the Battle of Pteria in 547 BC.

00:20:22.970 --> 00:20:26.380
Afterwards, he was decisively defeated at
the Battle of Thymbra.

00:20:26.380 --> 00:20:31.029
Croesus retreated to his capital of Sardis,
hoping to wait out the winter until his reinforcements

00:20:31.029 --> 00:20:32.029
arrived.

00:20:32.029 --> 00:20:36.429
Cyrus, however, followed him, laid siege on
Sardis and captured the capital after two

00:20:36.429 --> 00:20:37.429
weeks.

00:20:37.429 --> 00:20:41.080
Just like that, the Kingdom of Lydia had fallen
and was now part of the Achaemenid Empire.

00:20:41.080 --> 00:20:44.019
The fate of Croesus himself, however, is more
of a mystery.

00:20:44.019 --> 00:20:48.650
Ancient accounts are rather fanciful and claim
that the god Apollo came down from Olympus

00:20:48.650 --> 00:20:54.019
and saved the king before his execution and,
consequently, Cyrus accepted him as an advisor.

00:20:54.019 --> 00:20:58.860
Shockingly, modern historians don’t think
this actually happened, and many of them believe

00:20:58.860 --> 00:21:04.999
that Croesus did, indeed, die in 546 BC, after
the fall of Sardis, either by his own hand

00:21:04.999 --> 00:21:07.460
or by being burned on a pyre.

00:21:07.460 --> 00:21:12.049
According to Herodotus, while on the pyre,
Croesus cried out the name of Solon three

00:21:12.049 --> 00:21:13.049
times.

00:21:13.049 --> 00:21:17.279
Only then did he truly understand what the
wise Athenian tried to teach him, that no

00:21:17.279 --> 00:21:32.070
man is truly blessed until he dies well because,
until then, his fortune could change at any

00:21:32.070 --> 00:21:36.649
time.

